    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d819">

                <pb facs="0926" n="814" xml:id="pg814"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.52/357: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-07-03T15:00:00+08:00">July 3, 1935—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 3—6:47 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>334. Reference Canton’s despatch to the Legation No. 32 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >31</hi>], May 28, 1935 (copy sent direct to the Department)<anchor n="50"
                        xml:id="d819-f1"/> concerning temporary arrangement for stamping deeds at
                    Canton. In view of reservations made by Consulate General, Legation perceives no
                    objection to form of lease enclosed [in] that despatch and requests
                    authorization to instruct Canton that there would appear to be no objection to
                    Americans accepting such documents provided they do so voluntarily.<anchor
                        n="51" xml:id="d819-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d819-f1" n="50" xml:id="d819-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; for enclosure, see letter from the Consul General at Canton to the
                    Mayor of Canton, <ref target="#pg811">p. 811</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d819-f2" n="51" xml:id="d819-f2-content">
                <p>The Department replied by telegram No. 200, July 6, 2 p.m., approving the
                    instruction to Canton.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
